 ### [Aggregated Microfacet Distribution via Beta Distribution](10888-aggregated-microfacet-distribution-via-beta-distribution.md)
-This method models the aggregated microfacet roughness distribution $p_A(\alpha)$ using a Beta distribution $B(\alpha; a, b)$ to estimate the statistical spread of roughness values $\alpha$ from asset data. It is chosen over Gaussian approximations because the Beta distribution is naturally bounded within the valid $[0, 1]$ range of $\alpha$, preventing the invalid range errors that occur when Gaussian tails exceed these bounds. See [Aggregated Microfacet Distribution via Beta Distribution](10888-aggregated-microfacet-distribution-via-beta-distribution.md).
+This method models the aggregated microfacet roughness distribution $p_A(\alpha)$ using a Beta distribution $B(\alpha; a, b)$ to estimate the statistical spread of roughness values $\alpha$ from asset data. It sets itself apart from Gaussian approximations because the Beta distribution is naturally bounded within the valid $[0, 1]$ range of $\alpha$, preventing invalid range errors. Choose this method when the roughness distribution is strictly bounded and requires a statistically accurate representation. See [Aggregated Microfacet Distribution via Beta Distribution](10888-aggregated-microfacet-distribution-via-beta-distribution.md).
 
 ### [Aggregated Bidirectional Scattering Distribution Function (ABSDF)](13367-aggregated-bidirectional-scattering-distribution-function-ab.md)
-The Aggregated Bidirectional Scattering Distribution Function (ABSDF) is a method for efficient scene appearance aggregation for Level-of-Detail (LoD) rendering, designed to handle scenes with high geometric complexity and instancing. It defines a 4D function of incident and outgoing directions that describes the far-field appearance of a scene aggregate by integrating the 8D light transport function over positions, effectively capturing the aggregated appearance of all surfaces within a volume. Unlike explicit geometry rendering, ABSDF precomputes aggregated visibility and material properties, allowing the renderer to avoid tracing explicit geometry and shadow rays, thereby reducing the sample count required for convergence. It builds on [Aggregated Microfacet Distribution Approximation](10880-aggregated-microfacet-distribution-approximation.md) by providing a closed-form factorization that supports all-frequency and view-dependent effects. See [Aggregated Bidirectional Scattering Distribution Function (ABSDF)](13367-aggregated-bidirectional-scattering-distribution-function-ab.md).
+The Aggregated Bidirectional Scattering Distribution Function (ABSDF) is a method for efficient scene appearance aggregation for Level-of-Detail (LoD) rendering. It sets itself apart from explicit geometry rendering by precomputing aggregated visibility and material properties, allowing the renderer to avoid tracing explicit geometry and shadow rays. Choose this method when rendering scenes with high geometric complexity and instancing where explicit geometry tracing is too costly. See [Aggregated Bidirectional Scattering Distribution Function (ABSDF)](13367-aggregated-bidirectional-scattering-distribution-function-ab.md).
 
 ### [Horizon Principled BRDF](14801-horizon-principled-brdf.md)
-The Horizon Principled BRDF is a bidirectional reflectance distribution function used in the Horizon rendering engine, designed to be compatible with Disney's principled shading approach. It solves the problem of providing a physically plausible, energy-conserving shading model that supports a wide range of material types (dielectric, metallic, clear coat, sheen) through a unified set of normalized parameters. It sets itself apart from its parent, Aggregated Microfacet Distribution Approximation, by fixing specific parameter values (such as $\gamma=2$ for the main lobe and $\gamma=1$ for clear coat) and implementing a specific diffuse Fresnel variation to correct edge darkening, rather than using a general aggregation function. See [Horizon Principled BRDF](14801-horizon-principled-brdf.md).
